// A value (machine) call omits whenever it is NOT the root of the expression
// its construction site lowers. Any parent node over it is enough. Reduced
// from `samples/cli/probes/value_call_in_expr`.
//
//   let r: i32 = self.twelve();            checks   (call is the whole thing)
//   let r: i32 = (self.twelve());          checks   (parens are transparent)
//   let r: i32 = self.twelve() + 0;        OMITTED  (this fixture)
//   let r: i64 = self.twelve() as i64;     OMITTED  (a CAST parent, no
//                                                   arithmetic at all)
//   let f: bool = self.twelve() == 12;     OMITTED
//   let r: i32 = 0 - self.twelve();        OMITTED
//   let r: i32 = self.base + 0;            checks   (same arithmetic, no call)
//
// So it is neither the arithmetic nor the call, but the call having a parent.
//
// Ruled out, each by a checking control: the `in Saturating` policy (a
// policy-free program omits, and `in Wrapping` omits identically -- this is NOT
// another arithmetic-policy row removal, unlike the two composite gaps pinned
// beside it); the FIELD receiver (`self.double_val(..)` with no `Calc` type in
// the program omits); the call's ARGUMENT (a nullary callee omits); and the
// `let` binding itself -- a field store and a transition guard omit too, each
// at its own phase label, which is what says the label names the site rather
// than the cause.
//
// The sample's header attributes this to one historical miscompile,
// `self.base + self.calc.double_val(6) * 3`. The surviving trigger is far
// broader: ` + 0` alone reproduces it with nothing of that statement left.
//
// ONE SHARP ASYMMETRY, worth having before repairing. In a transition GUARD the
// left operand is a special case:
//
//   transition self.twelve() == 12 { .. }     checks
//   transition self.base == self.twelve() { } OMITTED
//   transition self.twelve() + z == 12 { }    OMITTED
//
// and the same depth-one comparison in a `let` initializer is not tolerated at
// all. So whatever admits the guard's left-hand call is a narrow special case,
// and generalizing it is the likely shape of the real repair.
use omega_language_std::console;
use omega::language::core::binding;

data Main {
    console: Binding<Console>;
    base: i32;
}

machine Main::twelve(&mut self) -> i32 {
    transition { _ -> (12) }
}

machine Main::main(&mut self) reaches Console {
    self.base = 1;
    transition { _ -> check() }

    state check(&mut self) {
        let result: i32 = self.twelve() + 0;
        transition { _ -> good() }
    }

    state good(&mut self) {
        self.console.exit_process(0);
    }
}
